"Traces true-positive rate against false-positive rate as a binary classifier’s decision threshold changes.\n\nUse when: A scoring classifier needs discrimination assessment across possible thresholds. Both positive and negative examples exist in a held-out evaluation sample.\n\nAvoid when: The decision depends mainly on precision under severe class imbalance without an accompanying precision-recall view.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes false-positive rate. Vertical position encodes true-positive rate; movement along the curve follows score thresholds.\n\nBuild: Define the positive class and obtain held-out continuous prediction scores. Calculate confusion counts at each distinct threshold. Draw the curve on fixed 0–1 axes with the chance diagonal and optional operating-point labels.\n\nCheck: Check higher scores mean stronger evidence for the declared positive class. Verify evaluation records were excluded from fitting and threshold selection as appropriate. State any averaging method and uncertainty around summary AUC.\n\nPitfalls: AUC hides which thresholds satisfy practical cost or capacity requirements. Good ROC performance does not guarantee calibrated probabilities or high precision at low prevalence.\n\nExample: Evaluate a synthetic defect classifier with held-out scores, showing the ROC curve and one explicitly chosen operating threshold.\n\nAlternatives: Precision-recall curve, Calibration curve, Error bar chart."
